switch: handoff notes
=====================

These notes carry the goal and the state of the work into another
project. Read them before changing anything.


1. The goal
-----------

A privacy WebExtension for GNU IceCat, called "switch", plus scripts
that configure an IceCat that is already installed. It must:

- look dark, in IceCat's style, with habamax colours (#1c1c1c ground,
  #bcbcbc text, #5f87af links, #af87af visited);
- be usable from the keyboard alone: every action has a key;
- switch JavaScript off and on, globally and per site;
- switch Tor on and off; with Tor on, every site gets its own Tor
  circuit (SOCKS username, IsolateSOCKSAuth) and its own fingerprint
  profile, tied together: one IP, one profile per site;
- answer every fingerprint probe with silence or a lie;
- wipe at quit every site that is not kept, with its profile; kept
  sites keep their data and their profile across restarts;
- show a "Sites" page with all data, profile and exit IP per site;
- look up each site's exit IP by itself (check.torproject.org, through
  that site's own circuit), again after ten minutes;
- be HTTPS-only, with a per-site list of sites allowed plain HTTP;
  .onion and local addresses are exempt;
- detect other add-ons holding the "proxy" permission, which can send
  traffic around Tor, and warn about them.

Cookie banners are NOT handled by switch: that is left to the add-on
"I don't care about cookies", which install.sh installs.


2. The rules the work follows
-----------------------------

- Plain files, small and self-contained, no build system, no
  dependencies beyond IceCat itself (and curl and sed for install.sh).
- ASCII only, English only, lines at most 80 columns, 4-space indents,
  C-like style even in JavaScript: declarations at the top of each
  function, one side effect per statement, braces as in K&R with
  function braces on their own lines.
- XHTML 1.0 Transitional pages, CSS 1 plus a little CSS 2.
- No telemetry, no update checks, no unique IDs; nothing identifying
  (names, hosts, paths of a real user) in code or examples.
- Our own data in flat formats only ("host secret" lines, one host
  per line), never nested JSON of our own design.
- Scripts never use sudo; they run as the user, or say clearly that
  root is needed.


3. Files
--------

icecat-switch/       the extension (manifest v2, version 0.12)
  manifest.json      permissions include proxy, webRequest(Blocking),
                     management, browsingData, cookies, contentScripts
  background.js      everything: settings, Tor routing (proxy.onRequest),
                     isolation, HTTPS upgrade, header lies, wipes, IP
                     lookups, rival detection, messages from the pages
  face.js            builds a site's profile from a seed (shared by the
                     background and the page script)
  inject.js          page script: the lies told inside each page
  popup.html/.js     the toolbar popup
  options.html/.js   settings, keys 1-6 jump to the sections
  sites.html/.js     the per-site data, profile and IP page
  nohttps.html/.js   shown when a site has no HTTPS: h once, a always,
                     b or Esc back
  style.css          shared dark style
  icon.svg
install.sh           configures an installed IceCat profile:
                     ./install.sh [-n] [-u] [-x] [-f] [-d] [profile]
                     -n dry run, -u uninstall, -x skip add-ons,
                     -f replace add-ons, -d fetch without Tor
                     env: TOR_SOCKS (127.0.0.1:9050), ICECAT_ROOT
icecat.prefs         130 prefs written to user.js; one per line,
                     "name value"; install.sh -u removes exactly these
addons.txt           GNUzilla catalogue numbers of the add-ons to fetch
                     (uBlock Origin, Dark Reader, I don't care about
                     cookies)
userChrome.css       habamax colours for IceCat's own window; replaced
                     only if absent or carrying the "switch: habamax"
                     marker
tidy.sh              removes the empty storage/default/<origin>
                     directories IceCat leaves behind, whose names leak
                     the sites visited: ./tidy.sh [-n] [-r] [profile]


4. Key design facts
-------------------

- Profiles: the seed is face_seed(secret, gen, site_gen, site). Kept
  hosts have their own lasting secret (state.faces, "host secret"
  lines); all other sites use the session secret, renewed at quit.
  New identity renews everything, kept hosts included.
- runtime.onStartup does not fire on the first start after an update,
  so the wipe of unkept sites runs whenever background.js loads.
- Another extension's proxy.onRequest can override ours; that is why
  management is used to find and name such rivals.
- install.sh sets network.proxy.failover_direct and allow_bypass to
  false, turns off HTTP/3 and DNS-over-HTTPS (trr.mode 5), so nothing
  escapes Tor; and keeps IceCat's own HTTPS-only mode off, because
  switch does it.
- install.sh refuses to run while that profile is in use (it reads the
  profile's "lock" link and checks the PID in it).
- GNUzilla's pages link fixed xpi files on addons.mozilla.org, from
  2023; install.sh fetches the page and the xpi through Tor, checks
  the add-on id, and copies it to extensions/<id>.xpi.


5. State
--------

Version 0.12 is written and passed: syntax checks, the 80-column and
ASCII checks, a unit test of the HTTPS upgrade logic, and a preview of
the popup with test data.

Not yet done or not yet checked:

- 0.12 is not installed: IceCat was running. Close IceCat and run
  ./install.sh.
- The "no HTTPS" page has not been seen with a real site lacking
  HTTPS; that needs a real IceCat.
- A loose switch.xpi (an old build) may still lie in the old project
  root; it can be deleted.


6. Ideas not yet taken up
-------------------------

- "I still don't care about cookies" (GNUzilla 2766861), the fork free
  of Avast, in place of the original; commented out in addons.txt.
- Tridactyl, for vim keys throughout IceCat.
- Bind "tidy.sh -r" to a key in dwm, so IceCat is always tidied after
  it quits.
